+| `--kv-window [auto\|N]` | the RESIDENT WINDOW of the attention KV, in cells: 'auto', '0' (off: fully resident), or N cells (default: 0).  The newest N cells stay in VRAM and everything older moves to pinned system RAM inside the SAME reservation -- one copy, no page table, no kernel change.  LOSSLESS and model-agnostic: nothing is dropped, a spilled cell is just read over the PCIe link, and that read costs decode in proportion to the depth past the window -- a window at or above the run depth costs nothing.  'auto' is sized by --fit; without --fit the cache declines it and says so.  OFF by default: a placement this consequential is an opt-in.  NOT the attendance pool (which keeps a device CACHE of a host-authoritative copy) and NOT the draft window (which TRUNCATES the drafter's cache).  LLAMA_KV_RESIDENT_CELLS is this option's deprecated alias<br/>(env: LLAMA_ARG_KV_WINDOW) |
+| `--kv-attendance-pool, --kv-region-pool [on\|off]` | take the ATTENDANCE POOL for the attention KV: <bool> = on/off (default: off).  The KV's AUTHORITATIVE copy moves to a pinned host mirror and the device keeps a compact pool of indexer pages plus a page table, which the sparse QSA decode resolves through (a miss is one DMA fill, then it hits).  Only a qwen4exp trunk can take it (a uniform indexer-block geometry, one stream, device-offloaded K/V); it declines everywhere else, on an integrated GPU, and while the resident window is active -- the two are ALTERNATIVES for the same bytes (cache-and-fill vs spill-and-read), never layers.  It buys VRAM (a ~204 MiB pool instead of a 1.6-2.4 GiB KV) but costs prompt throughput on a KV that fits (~48 % of pp32K), so it is a fittability feature and OFF by default.  The pool's SIZE is derived from the model (one decode band of the indexer working set); a legacy numeric size or 'auto' is accepted for compatibility and IGNORED<br/>(env: LLAMA_ARG_KV_REGION_POOL) |
