+| `-lme, --limit-host-experts auto\|mirror\|N` | cap the NON-SWAPPABLE memory spent on MoE experts that are not in VRAM (default: auto)<br/>On a discrete GPU (host-resident experts, --n-cpu-moe/-ncmoe):<br/>- auto: pin the whole host expert set when it fits MOE_HOST_LIMIT_FRAC (default 80%) of the available<br/>        memory; otherwise no pinned master -- the experts stay in the read-only model mapping and the<br/>        bounded pinned pool (MOE_HOST_POOL_MIB/MOE_HOST_POOL_FRAC) serves the reads.  It is decided at<br/>        load, before any tensor is placed, and logged once.<br/>- N:    pin N GiB of host experts, spent on WHOLE tensors in descending hotness order (the persisted<br/>        expert heat map ranks them, so the bytes that are actually read are the ones that are pinned).<br/>        The rest stays in the model mapping.  An explicit N is honoured EXACTLY: the pool's<br/>        MOE_HOST_POOL_RAM_PCT ceiling scales only a DERIVED budget, never a number you typed.<br/>        N is refused only at or above 100% of the memory currently available (that memory cannot be<br/>        swapped out); between 85% and 100% it is accepted with a warning, and if headroom runs out the<br/>        pool's prewarm stops placing and the rest grows on demand.<br/>- mirror: the WHOLE host expert set, explicitly -- a covering pool, every expert resident (h=1.0000).<br/>        This is what `auto` resolves to on a box with memory to spare, but stated as a request instead of<br/>        inferred: neither MOE_HOST_LIMIT_FRAC nor MOE_HOST_POOL_RAM_PCT may scale it, and it is not<br/>        refused for exceeding the memory available -- an explicit mirror that does not fit is the<br/>        operator's call, not something to second-guess.<br/>On an integrated GPU (APU, one shared host/device memory pool) this is the SIZE OF THE REGISTERED GTT<br/>EXPERT STORE: a registered, huge-page region of host memory that the GPU reads in place, sliced into<br/>per-(tensor, expert) slots.  No -ncmoe is needed (host-expert offload is refused there), and the same<br/>budget and the same heat ranking apply -- N GiB spent on the hottest expert tables, the rest stays<br/>on its layer's device buffer:<br/>- auto: the whole expert set when it fits MOE_HOST_LIMIT_FRAC of MemAvailable (then nothing is ever<br/>        evicted), otherwise that ceiling as a bounded hot subset.<br/>- N:    exactly N GiB of registered GTT store (capped at the set).  N is refused only at or above 100%<br/>        of the memory currently available, for the same reason as above.<br/>- mirror: the whole expert set, explicitly (the physical lock/budget ceilings still apply to the<br/>        registered region, because it must be lockable to be a reservation).<br/>The store must be LOCKED to be a reservation: a registered-but-unlocked region is reclaimable under<br/>memory pressure (pages swapped, huge pages split, the next read 12-37x slower -- measured).  So the<br/>extent is clamped to RLIMIT_MEMLOCK (`ulimit -l`), and a model that only fits because of the store is<br/>REFUSED when it cannot be locked at all, rather than silently degrading.  `-lme auto` sizes to whatever<br/>the limit allows.  MOE_EXPERT_GTT=0 disables the store on an APU; MOE_EXPERT_GTT_MLOCK=0 leaves it<br/>unlocked (diagnostic: reclaimable by design).  (--host-experts pinned\|pool are deprecated shorthands<br/>for the same budget.) |
